source: application log during container startup
produced-by: docs/migration/smoke-evidence/smoke-checks.sh
region-status: SKIPPED
skip-reason: no six-service reference stack and no servlet container on this host,
  therefore no container startup log from which an annotation-scanning region
  could be selected.  Measured on this host rather than assumed, and every value
  below was read back before this file was written: the hostname arkcase-ce.local
  does not resolve, and anonymous probes of the application context and of the
  search service each returned 000, meaning no HTTP response was received at all
  rather than an error response; TOMCAT_HOME, CATALINA_HOME and CATALINA_LOG are
  all unset and no catalina.sh is on PATH, so there is no servlet container to
  start; and a search of the whole working tree found zero .war files, so there is
  no archive to deploy.  A further, independent blocker: the designated producer
  named above refuses to start unless an administrator credential is supplied
  through its environment, so no run of it was possible here even setting the
  absent stack aside, and supplying administrator material would have been neither
  available nor appropriate.  The older runtime is deliberately NOT listed among
  the unmet preconditions, because it is not one: a JDK 8 installation is present
  on this host and answers openjdk version "1.8.0_492", and the sibling
  env/toolchain.txt records that same banner for the baseline side.  What is
  missing is the deployment and its backing services, not the JVM.
normalised: nothing, because no bytes were captured.  The policy is nevertheless
  stated in full, so that the migrated counterpart reproduces the same
  transformation rather than inventing one, and so that a reader can see which
  differences between the two sides would have been suppressed and which would
  have been allowed to show.  When this region is captured the pipeline strips
  timestamps and dates, thread names and thread identifiers, session identifiers,
  generated request and instance identifiers, absolute filesystem paths in favour
  of a stable token, port assignments, and elapsed-time and duration figures.  It
  preserves the behaviour-bearing content, which in this region is unusually
  concentrated: the scanned package and URL names, the scanned-type counts, the
  discovered class and interface names, the names of the consuming components, and
  the full message text of every warning and error line.  Over-normalising this
  region would destroy the very evidence it exists to carry, because here the
  counts and the class names ARE the behaviour.
  One tension inside this region is resolved deliberately rather than left to
  chance.  Classpath scanning output routinely carries absolute jar and directory
  paths, and those paths are volatile between two hosts.  The volatile absolute
  prefix is replaced with a stable token, and the artefact name and the package
  name inside that path are PRESERVED, because which artefact was scanned and
  which package was found are behaviour and the directory it happened to sit in is
  not.  A blanket path rewrite that swallowed the artefact name would leave this
  region with nothing to compare.
redacted: nothing, because no bytes were captured.  When it is captured, every
  credential-shaped value is replaced with the fixed placeholder <REDACTED>, which
  is the convention this documentation tree already uses and which the repository
  itself already applies.  Scanning output is not a usual carrier of credentials,
  but two carriers reach this region in practice and both are treated identically:
  a datasource or directory-service credential echoed by a component whose
  initialisation interleaves with a scan, and keystore or trust-store passwords
  echoed by a container that prints its own launch arguments, which are recorded
  only in the redacted forms keyStorePassword=<REDACTED> and
  trustStorePassword=<REDACTED>.  The documented administrator credential is never
  transcribed here in any form, not even as an illustration of what redaction
  removes.
