----- END CAPTURE -----
notes:
- The capture delimiters above are deliberately empty.  Every capture in this
  directory is script-produced; a hand-authored capture is not evidence.  Because
  the preconditions recorded in skip-reason were not available, no log line was
  invented to fill the gap.  A fabricated baseline would make every
  behaviour-preserved claim in this migration unfalsifiable, which defeats the
  baseline-as-tie-breaker rule outright.  An explicit, machine-readable skip with a
  reason is rule-compliant; an invented capture is not, and is strictly worse than
  an honest absence.
- A SKIPPED region is NOT a pass, and two matching SKIPPED regions across baseline
  and migrated are NOT evidence of preserved behaviour.  They record an absence in
  both places.  The sibling notes/flows-not-captured.txt reaches the same verdict
  for the eight application flows; this region is consistent with that record
  rather than an exception to it.
- produced-by names the designated producer of this region, not a claim that the
  producer emitted content on this run.  It emitted none, which is why the
  delimiters are empty.  Its startup-region path is driven by an optional container
  log supplied through its environment; when that log is unset or unreadable it
  writes an explicit unavailability record and returns without mining any region at
  all.  That is exactly what happened here, and the record it left is the sibling
  unavailable.log in this same directory.
- Region-selection rule, stated here so the migrated capture reproduces it without
  guesswork rather than selecting a different slice of the same log.  From the
  first-startup window, select every line emitted by the reflection-based
  classpath scanning library and every line emitted by the seven consuming
  components named below while a scan is in progress: the packages or classpath
  URLs handed to each scanner, the scanner types configured, the counts and names
  of the types, fields and methods discovered, the successful completion of each
  scan, and every warning or error line the scanning library raises.  Select
  nothing else.  The window is the same window every sibling startup region is
  drawn from, so the regions partition that window instead of overlapping it.
- Selection boundary that is easy to get wrong in both directions.  Selecting only
  lines whose logger belongs to the scanning library would silently drop the
  consuming components' own before-and-after lines, which is where the discovered
  counts actually surface; and selecting the whole context-initialisation window
  would duplicate a sibling region and make the mirror diff report the same bytes
  twice.  The rule above is written to the components rather than to the logger for
  that reason.
- Why this region exists at all, stated precisely.  The reflection-based scanning
  library reads RAW CLASS BYTES to find annotations and sub-types.  That makes it a
  textbook candidate blocker on a new class-file major version, and it is
  thoroughly load-bearing in this application, including underneath the permission
  evaluator.  It was nevertheless deliberately HELD at its existing version, on the
  strength of the migration's own assessment — an end-to-end scan of a class file at
  major version 61 succeeded on the installed release with its existing companions.
  That assessment is cited here as the migration's finding, not as something this
  capture witnessed; what this capture contributes is the baseline half of the
  comparison the assessment is checked against.  Holding a version after assessment
  is as much a rule-compliant decision as changing one, and it needs evidence too.
  This region is that evidence on the baseline side.
- Governing rule on justified changes, the rule this region evidences, quoted from
  the requirements: "Every dependency version change must have a specific Java 17
  or Node 20 compatibility reason... A change without a reason is out of scope."
  Its CONTRAPOSITIVE is what applies here, and it is load-bearing rather than a
  debating point: bumping the scanning library or its class-parsing companion
  without a demonstrated incompatibility would ITSELF violate that rule.  Both were
  therefore assessed and both were held.
- Measured evidence that holding was the decision, re-derived at the base commit and
  again against the current working tree so that the pair can be compared.  The
  scanning library's version property reads 0.9.9 at pom.xml:136 at the base commit,
  with its managed entry at pom.xml:1642-1645; the same property still reads 0.9.9
  after the migration, at the managed entry pom.xml:1999-2003 in the current tree.
  Identical value on both sides, which is the whole point: a held version is
  verifiable by comparison and does not have to be taken on trust.
  command: git show c8f6226105:pom.xml | grep -o '<reflections.version>[^<]*<'
  command: grep -o '<reflections.version>[^<]*<' pom.xml
- The class-parsing companion is not declared by this repository at all, at either
  revision, and that is worth recording because it changes where a reviewer should
  look.  The scanning library's own published POM declares the companion as a
  non-optional dependency at 3.18.2-GA, so that is the version which arrives
