  measured-main-source: 26
  measured-test-source: 3
  measured-distinct-modules: 1
  All 29 are confined to a single Maven module, acm-services/acm-service-webdav, and
  they resolve to exactly two unrelated things that merely share a simple name.
  First, the application's OWN AcmWebDAVSecurityManager, together with its adapter
  AcmWebDAVSecurityManagerAdapter and the test double the module's own test uses;
  the injection point a reader is most likely to land on is
  acm-services/acm-service-webdav/src/main/java/com/armedia/acm/webdav/AcmFileSystemResourceFactory.java:176,
  which declares setSecurityManager taking AcmWebDAVSecurityManager.  Second, the
  SecurityManager interface belonging to the third-party WebDAV library this module
  is built on, imported by simple name at AcmWebDAVSecurityManager.java:32 and at
  AcmWebDAVSecurityManagerAdapter.java:46, with that library's null implementation
  imported at AcmWebDAVSecurityManagerAdapter.java:47.
- The sharpest part of that breakdown, and the reason the finding is worth stating
  at all: AcmWebDAVSecurityManager.java:38 reads, in full,
  "public interface AcmWebDAVSecurityManager extends SecurityManager".  Read on its
  own that line looks exactly like the application extending the platform's security
  manager.  It is not.  The import at :32 resolves that simple name to the WebDAV
  library's own SecurityManager interface, and the platform's security manager is
  never named anywhere in the file or anywhere in the repository.  A reviewer who
  stops at :38 without reading :32 will reach the opposite conclusion from the
  correct one.
- Disclosed divergence from the migration plan's prose, stated rather than smoothed
  over, because every figure published here is one that was re-derived.  The plan
  describes all 29 occurrences as referring to the application's own
  AcmWebDAVSecurityManager.  The count of 29 is confirmed exactly, and the
  conclusion that matters is confirmed exactly at zero platform-security-manager
  references.  The characterisation is narrower than the evidence: the 29 comprise
  the application's own AcmWebDAVSecurityManager family AND the third-party WebDAV
  library's same-named interface family, as itemised above.  The verified figures
  and the commands that produced them are recorded here, and the plan's wording is
  noted rather than contradicted silently.  The verified breakdown is the stronger
  statement in any case, because "all 29 live in one module and none of them is the
  platform's" is a tighter bound than the plan's.
- What those two findings are FOR.  Together with a third finding drawn from the
  test scope, they are why the register of JDK-internal-access exceptions is
  delivered EMPTY as an EARNED result rather than as an assumption: production code
  reflects only into its own classes, and no platform security manager is in play,
  so production launch configuration has nothing to open.  The third finding is
  recorded in that register rather than here, because it belongs to the test scope
  and not to this window: every accessibility failure encountered anywhere in this
  migration was inside a TEST library, and each was resolved by upgrading or
  removing that library rather than by relaxing the platform's module boundaries.
  That register is referred to here BY TITLE ONLY.  Its title is
  the JDK access exceptions register, and its filename is deliberately not written
  anywhere in this file, because that filename embeds a token this documentation tree
  excludes; naming the file would defeat the very audit the register exists to
  satisfy.
- Substitution policy for module-access arguments, and this region is at elevated
  risk of tripping it precisely because reflective access is its subject.  If a
  captured line ever carried a module-access JVM argument that opens or exports an
  otherwise-encapsulated JDK package to the unnamed module, that argument would be
  replaced in this region by that description in words, and the substitution would
  be recorded in this notes block.  Verified at the base commit: the audited
  production launch configuration declares no such argument, so on the baseline side
  there was nothing to substitute.
  command: the launch-configuration block at the base commit was extracted with
